--TEST--
Core\Db::inList refuses an empty list rather than picking between "match nothing" and "match everything", and the refusal is the length alone — ADR 0067 § 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5: an empty list matches nothing inside `IN` and everything inside
// `NOT IN`, the rewriter cannot tell which it is in, and silently picking one
// is worse than making the caller branch. So the refusal lands at the *call* —
// where the caller who wrote the empty list is still on the stack — rather
// than at the bind, where the statement is but that caller is not.

array<int> $none = [];
try {
    Core\Db::inList($none);
    echo "an empty list was accepted\n";
} catch (LogicError $empty) {
    echo $empty->message, "\n";
}

// The refusal is the list's length and nothing else about it: a run of one
// `null` is still a run, because `NULL` is a value a placeholder binds.
array<?int> $maybe = [null];
Core\Db::inList($maybe);
echo "a run of one null is not an empty list\n";

// And the branch § 5 asks for is one the caller writes around the call, which
// is why this is a `LogicError` and not a value the member could have chosen.
if (Core\Arr::isEmpty($none)) {
    echo "the caller branches\n";
} else {
    Core\Db::inList($none);
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Db::inList() was given an empty list, and ADR 0067 § 5 refuses one: an empty list matches nothing inside `IN` and everything inside `NOT IN`, the rewriter cannot tell which it is in, and the caller has to branch
a run of one null is not an empty list
the caller branches
